    {
      "@id": "urn:rkaf:fragment:urn%3Arulespec%3Adocument-understanding%3Adocument%3A4d53ee5bb82b4562bf890b41c6bf9c154ada3917edc5705a3a91d12a43964493:14300:16129:sha256-694144d9e1ba19dd6517077af028c3e58838e40224e655f9e9a8f41b1f5152f7",
      "@type": "rkaf:SourceFragment",
      "oa:hasSource": "urn:rulespec:document-understanding:document:4d53ee5bb82b4562bf890b41c6bf9c154ada3917edc5705a3a91d12a43964493",
      "rkaf:fragmentIdentityScheme": "rkaf:carrier-local-fragment",
      "rkaf:sourceArtifactDigest": "sha256:4d53ee5bb82b4562bf890b41c6bf9c154ada3917edc5705a3a91d12a43964493",
      "rkaf:fragmentContentDigest": "sha256:694144d9e1ba19dd6517077af028c3e58838e40224e655f9e9a8f41b1f5152f7",
      "rkaf:selectorKind": [
        "oa:TextQuoteSelector",
        "oa:TextPositionSelector"
      ],
      "oa:hasSelector": [
        {
          "@type": "oa:TextQuoteSelector",
          "oa:exact": "(ii) What is “first aid”? For the purposes of part 1904, “first aid” means the following:\n\n(A) Using a non-prescription medication at nonprescription strength (for medications available in both prescription and non-prescription form, a recommendation by a physician or other licensed health care professional to use a non-prescription medication at prescription strength is considered medical treatment for recordkeeping purposes);\n\n(B) Administering tetanus immunizations (other immunizations, such as Hepatitis B vaccine or rabies vaccine, are considered medical treatment);\n\n(C) Cleaning, flushing or soaking wounds on the surface of the skin;\n\n(D) Using wound coverings such as bandages, Band-Aids TM , gauze pads, etc.; or using butterfly bandages or Steri-Strips TM (other wound closing devices such as sutures, staples, etc., are considered medical treatment);\n\n(E) Using hot or cold therapy;\n\n(F) Using any non-rigid means of support, such as elastic bandages, wraps, non-rigid back belts, etc. (devices with rigid stays or other systems designed to immobilize parts of the body are considered medical treatment for recordkeeping purposes);\n\n(G) Using temporary immobilization devices while transporting an accident victim ( e.g., splints, slings, neck collars, back boards, etc.).\n\n(H) Drilling of a fingernail or toenail to relieve pressure, or draining fluid from a blister;\n\n(I) Using eye patches;\n\n(J) Removing foreign bodies from the eye using only irrigation or a cotton swab;\n\n(K) Removing splinters or foreign material from areas other than the eye by irrigation, tweezers, cotton swabs or other simple means;\n\n(L) Using finger guards;\n\n(M) Using massages (physical therapy or chiropractic treatment are considered medical treatment for recordkeeping purposes); or\n\n(N) Drinking fluids for relief of heat stress."
        },
        {
          "@type": "oa:TextPositionSelector",
          "oa:start": 14300,
          "oa:end": 16129,
          "rkaf:coordinateSystem": "rkaf:unicode-codepoint"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "urn:rulespec:document-understanding:assertion:e0812b787255eb6c98bab4e20c6f3637db89bce3e5267fbe2c64976141e30633",
      "@type": "rkaf:ValueAssertion",
      "rkaf:assertionOrigin": "rkaf:aiSuggested",
